/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:72:128,Genos: a human-centric genomic foundation model.,"Abstract Background The rapid expansion of human genomic data demands foundation models that manage ultra-long sequences and capture population diversity, limitations common in existing models that lack human-specific representation, and clinical inference efficiency. Results Here, we introduce Genos (Genos-1.2B/Genos-10B), a human-centric genomic foundation model engineered for million-basepair sequence modeling. Genos utilizes a large-scale mixture of experts structure, optimized for a 1-Mb context, trained on high-quality human de novo assemblies from datasets such as the Human Pangenome Reference Consortium and the Human Genome Structural Variation Consortium, representing diverse global populations. A suite of optimization strategies was implemented to ensure training stability and enhance computational efficiency, which collectively reduces costs and facilitates million-basepair context modeling. Functionally, Genos performs single-nucleotide resolution analysis and dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations on RNA expression profiles. In comprehensive evaluations, Genos uniformly surpasses state-of-the-art models on critical human genomics benchmarks and demonstrates robust omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities. We present a systematic technical evaluation and validation of Genos’s architecture, training convergence, and performance across standard benchmarks. Conclusions This work provides a reliable technical blueprint and performance benchmark for the development of the next generation of high-efficiency genomic foundation models. Genos model weights, inference code, and usage documentation are publicly available on GitHub (https://github.com/BGI-HangzhouAI/Genos) and Hugging Face Hub (https://huggingface.co/BGI-HangzhouAI).",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Genos: a human-centric genomic foundation model.,"Abstract Background The rapid expansion of human genomic data demands foundation models that manage ultra-long sequences and capture population diversity, limitations common in existing models that lack human-specific representation, and clinical inference efficiency. Results Here, we introduce Genos (Genos-1.2B/Genos-10B), a human-centric genomic foundation model engineered for million-basepair sequence modeling. Genos utilizes a large-scale mixture of experts structure, optimized for a 1-Mb context, trained on high-quality human de novo assemblies from datasets such as the Human Pangenome Reference Consortium and the Human Genome Structural Variation Consortium, representing diverse global populations. A suite of optimization strategies was implemented to ensure training stability and enhance computational efficiency, which collectively reduces costs and facilitates million-basepair context modeling. Functionally, Genos performs single-nucleotide resolution analysis and dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations on RNA expression profiles. In comprehensive evaluations, Genos uniformly surpasses state-of-the-art models on critical human genomics benchmarks and demonstrates robust omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities. We present a systematic technical evaluation and validation of Genos’s architecture, training convergence, and performance across standard benchmarks. Conclusions This work provides a reliable technical blueprint and performance benchmark for the development of the next generation of high-efficiency genomic foundation models. Genos model weights, inference code, and usage documentation are publicly available on GitHub (https://github.com/BGI-HangzhouAI/Genos) and Hugging Face Hub (https://huggingface.co/BGI-HangzhouAI).",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"Genos is a genomic sequence foundation model with no evidence of natural-language text input, output, or conditioning; the vague 'omics-text cross-modal' phrase does not provide sufficient evidence for a text component or text-bio bridge. Additionally, no generative mechanism (e.g., autoregressive, diffusion) is described, so it does not meet the generative model criterion.","The abstract presents Genos as a genomic foundation model with a brief mention of 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' but provides no detail on natural-language text input/output or conditioning, leaving the text component and text-bio bridge unclear.","Abstract describes a genomic foundation model with mixture-of-experts and million-basepair context, but does not explicitly state a generative architecture (e.g., autoregressive, decoder, diffusion). Generative status is unclear.","Genos is a genomic sequence foundation model with no evidence of natural-language text input, output, or conditioning; the vague 'omics-text cross-modal' phrase does not provide sufficient evidence for a text component or text-bio bridge. Additionally, no generative mechanism (e.g., autoregressive, diffusion) is described, so it does not meet the generative model criterion.",Genos: a human-centric genomic foundation model.,"Abstract Background The rapid expansion of human genomic data demands foundation models that manage ultra-long sequences and capture population diversity, limitations common in existing models that lack human-specific representation, and clinical inference efficiency. Results Here, we introduce Genos (Genos-1.2B/Genos-10B), a human-centric genomic foundation model engineered for million-basepair sequence modeling. Genos utilizes a large-scale mixture of experts structure, optimized for a 1-Mb context, trained on high-quality human de novo assemblies from datasets such as the Human Pangenome Reference Consortium and the Human Genome Structural Variation Consortium, representing diverse global populations. A suite of optimization strategies was implemented to ensure training stability and enhance computational efficiency, which collectively reduces costs and facilitates million-basepair context modeling. Functionally, Genos performs single-nucleotide resolution analysis and dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations on RNA expression profiles. In comprehensive evaluations, Genos uniformly surpasses state-of-the-art models on critical human genomics benchmarks and demonstrates robust omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities. We present a systematic technical evaluation and validation of Genos’s architecture, training convergence, and performance across standard benchmarks. Conclusions This work provides a reliable technical blueprint and performance benchmark for the development of the next generation of high-efficiency genomic foundation models. Genos model weights, inference code, and usage documentation are publicly available on GitHub (https://github.com/BGI-HangzhouAI/Genos) and Hugging Face Hub (https://huggingface.co/BGI-HangzhouAI).",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,unclear,yes,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract introduces Genos as a genomic foundation model but only ambiguously references 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' without clear natural-language text or text-bio alignment. No explicit generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive) is described. These unresolved criteria make the record a borderline case due to insufficient abstract detail.","The abstract presents Genos as a genomic foundation model for sequence modeling, with a brief mention of 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' but no clear description of a text component or text-bio bridge as part of the model itself.","The abs