tegies was implemented to ensure training stability and enhance computational efficiency, which collectively reduces costs and facilitates million-basepair context modeling. Functionally, Genos performs single-nucleotide resolution analysis and dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations on RNA expression profiles. In comprehensive evaluations, Genos uniformly surpasses state-of-the-art models on critical human genomics benchmarks and demonstrates robust omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities. We present a systematic technical evaluation and validation of Genos’s architecture, training convergence, and performance across standard benchmarks. Conclusions This work provides a reliable technical blueprint and performance benchmark for the development of the next generation of high-efficiency genomic foundation models. Genos model weights, inference code, and usage documentation are publicly available on GitHub (https://github.com/BGI-HangzhouAI/Genos) and Hugging Face Hub (https://huggingface.co/BGI-HangzhouAI).",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,unclear,yes,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract introduces Genos as a genomic foundation model but only ambiguously references 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' without clear natural-language text or text-bio alignment. No explicit generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive) is described. These unresolved criteria make the record a borderline case due to insufficient abstract detail.","The abstract presents Genos as a genomic foundation model for sequence modeling, with a brief mention of 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' but no clear description of a text component or text-bio bridge as part of the model itself.","The abstract presents a 