/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1621:            "evidence_quote": "each training sample was organized into the format '[Original Text] TL;DR: [Summary]'",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1637:          "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1640:          "DNA Sequence Data"
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1642:        "heading": "DNA Sequence Data",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1670:        "text": "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs\n1. Construction of the Pre-trained Model gpt2-gene-eng\n2.2. Pretrained model\nDNA Sequence Data\nWe followed the pre-training data approach used by DNABERT, extracting fragments ranging from 300 to 1000 base pairs (bp) from multiple model organisms. The total volume of DNA sequence data is approximately 10 GB, with 2 GB of this data randomly selected for training the tokenizer.",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1741:            "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1744:            "DNA Sequence Data"
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1746:          "heading": "DNA Sequence Data",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1774:          "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1807:        "text": "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs\n1. Construction of the Pre-trained Model gpt2-gene-eng\n2.2. Pretrained model\nProtein Sequence Data\nFrom the UniProt database, we extracted 10 GB of protein sequence data, including all entries from Swiss-Prot and randomly selected entries from TrEMBL.",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1878:            "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:1911:          "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2022:        "text": "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs\n1. Construction of the Pre-trained Model gpt2-gene-eng\n2.2. Pretrained model\nNatural Language Data\nWe primarily used the OpenWebText dataset, combined with Wikipedia data, as the natural language training dataset, focusing on English text. From these datasets, we extracted 10 GB of data for pretraining purposes.\nOne important condition for achieving multilingual transfer ability is the use of a unied tokenizer, typically  based  on  the  Byte  Pair  Encoding  (BPE)  method.  In  this  study,  we  trained  a  BPE  tokenizer from scratch using DNA, protein, and English data, resulting in a vocabulary of approximately 100,000 tokens. For  the  model,  we  employed  the  GPT2-small  architecture  and  trained  a  GPT2  model  from  scratch using DNA, protein, and English data, referred to as gpt2-gene-eng . The specic network structure is detailed as follows:\n7\nFigure 2. Structure of gpt2-gene-eng . The model architecture is identical to that of GPT-2 small. Pretraining and instruction tuning use the same causal language header, the layer mapped 768 hidden units to 100000 units (the number of tokens in the vocabulary). Classication uses sequence classication header, The layer maps from 768 hidden units to only 2 units, where the 2 units represent the two classes id.\nAccording  to  the  typical  design  of  GPT-2,  it  accepts  sequences  with  a  maximum  length  of  1024  as input.  We used the same architecture as the GPT-2 Small model, which consists of 12 Transformer layers, each with 768 hidden units and 12 attention heads. This small model has approximately 117 million parameters.We trained the GPT-2 model using mixed-precision oating-point arithmetic on a machine equipped with a single Nvidia 4090 GPU. We employed a dynamic learning rate schedule, and the model was trained for a total of 3 to 5 epochs.",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2093:            "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2126:          "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2197:        "text": "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs\n1. Construction of the Pre-trained Model gpt2-gene-eng\n2.2. Pretrained model\nThe training data for is roughly outlined in the table below:\nTable 1. Training data\nThe training data for the paper is structured as follows:",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2268:            "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2276:          "text": "The training data for is roughly outlined in the table below:\n\nTable 1. Training data\n\nThe training data for the paper is structured as follows:\n\n##### DNA Sequence Data\n\nWe followed the pre-training data approach used by DNABERT, extracting fragments ranging from 300 to 1000 base pairs (bp) from multiple model organisms. The total volume of DNA sequence data is approximately 10 GB, with 2 GB of this data randomly selected for training the tokenizer.\n\n##### Protein Sequence Data\n\nFrom the UniProt database, we extracted 10 GB of protein sequence data, including all entries from Swiss-Prot and randomly selected entries from TrEMBL.\n\n##### Natural Language Data\n\nWe primarily used the OpenWebText dataset, combined with Wikipedia data, as the natural language training dataset, focusing on English text. From these datasets, we extracted 10 GB of data for pretraining purposes.\n\nOne important condition for achieving multilingual transfer ability is the use of a unied tokenizer, typically  based  on  the  Byte  Pair  Encoding  (BPE)  method.  In  this  study,  we  trained  a  BPE  tokenizer from scratch using DNA, protein, and English data, resulting in a vocabulary of approximately 100,000 tokens. For  the  model,  we  employed  the  GPT2-small  architecture  and  trained  a  GPT2  model  from  scratch using DNA, protein, and English data, referred to as gpt2-gene-eng . The specic network structure is detailed as follows:\n\n7\n\nFigure 2. Structure of gpt2-gene-eng . The model architecture is identical to that of GPT-2 small. Pretraining and instruction tuning use the same causal language header, the layer mapped 768 hidden units to 100000 units (the number of tokens in the vocabulary). Classication uses sequence classication header, The layer maps from 768 hidden units to only 2 units, where the 2 units represent the two classes id.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nAccording  to  the  typical  design  of  GPT-2,  it  accepts  sequences  with  a  maximum  length  of  1024  as input.  We used the same architecture as the GPT-2 Small model, which consists of 12 Transformer layers, each with 768 hidden units and 12 attention heads. This small model has approximately 117 million parameters.We trained the GPT-2 model using mixed-precision oating-point arithmetic on a machine equipped with a single Nvidia 4090 GPU. We employed a dynamic learning rate schedule, and the model was trained for a total of 3 to 5 epochs.",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2300:          "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2351:        "text": "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs\n2. Binary Classication Problem\nModel Training\nWe  used  the  GPT2LMHeadModel  with  a  causal  language  modeling  head  as  the  base  model  for paragraph  prediction.  The  training  process  was  based  on  a  language  modeling  task,  employing continuous  pre-training  to  enable  the  model  to  learn  the  patterns  of  paragraph  boundaries.  Postpreprocessing, texts marked with <p_end> were used as training inputs, with the model's objective being  to  predict  the  next  token,  including  both  regular  vocabulary  and  the  paragraph  end  marker <p_end>. Through this approach, the task of predicting paragraph boundaries was integrated into the language modeling task without the need to design additional task heads.\nDuring training, we employed cross-entropy as the loss function and introduced a dynamic learning rate  adjustment  strategy  to  optimize  convergence  speed.  We  chose  the  AdamW  optimizer  with  an initial learning rate set at 5×10-55×10-5. The model learned the patterns of paragraph boundaries on the training set while evaluating the accuracy and uency of generated text on the validation set. After several  rounds  of  training,  the  model  was  able  to  capture  contextual  information  and  accurately generate paragraph end markers.",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2395:            "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2432:          "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2452:          "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2460:        "text": "The training data for is roughly outlined in the table below:\n\nTable 1. Training data\n\nThe training data for the paper is structured as follows:\n\n##### DNA Sequence Data\n\nWe followed the pre-training data approach used by DNABERT, extracting fragments ranging from 300 to 1000 base pairs (bp) from multiple model organisms. The total volume of DNA sequence data is approximately 10 GB, with 2 GB of this data randomly selected for training the tokenizer.\n\n##### Protein Sequence Data\n\nFrom the UniProt database, we extracted 10 GB of protein sequence data, including all entries from Swiss-Prot and randomly selected entries from TrEMBL.\n\n##### Natural Language Data\n\nWe primarily used the OpenWebText dataset, combined with Wikipedia data, as the natural language training dataset, focusing on English text. From these datasets, we extracted 10 GB of data for pretraining purposes.\n\nOne important condition for achieving multilingual transfer ability is the use of a unied tokenizer, typically  based  on  the  Byte  Pair  Encoding  (BPE)  method.  In  this  study,  we  trained  a  BPE  tokenizer from scratch using DNA, protein, and English data, resulting in a vocabulary of approximately 100,000 tokens. For  the  model,  we  employed  the  GPT2-small  architecture  and  trained  a  GPT2  model  from  scratch using DNA, protein, and English data, referred to as gpt2-gene-eng . The specic network structure is detailed as follows:\n\n7\n\nFigure 2. Structure of gpt2-gene-eng . The model architecture is identical to that of GPT-2 small. Pretraining and instruction tuning use the same causal language header, the layer mapped 768 hidden units to 100000 units (the number of tokens in the vocabulary). Classication uses sequence classication header, The layer maps from 768 hidden units to only 2 units, where the 2 units represent the two classes id.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nAccording  to  the  typical  design  of  GPT-2,  it  accepts  sequences  with  a  maximum  length  of  1024  as input.  We used the same architecture as the GPT-2 Small model, which consists of 12 Transformer layers, each with 768 hidden units and 12 attention heads. This small model has approximately 117 million parameters.We trained the GPT-2 model using mixed-precision oating-point arithmetic on a machine equipped with a single Nvidia 4090 GPU. We employed a dynamic learning rate schedule, and the model was trained for a total of 3 to 5 epochs.",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2471:          "Human Genome Book: Words, Sentences and Paragraphs",
/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_07/full_2026-07-06__rec_002243/screening_evidence_summary.json:2474:          "DNA Sequence Data"
